#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133605 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "a4eeb5c3-c1d5-5eb2-bb4e-03de915e0dab" :question/math-academy-id "e-8579" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle {x(t)}, {y(t)} \\rangle   = x(0)y(0) + x(1)y(1) + x(-1)y(-1).\n$$\n\nGiven that $p(t) = 1 + t + t^2$ and $q(t) = kt - t^2$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)+p(-1)q(-1) \\\\  & =(1)(0)+(3)(k-1)+(1)(-k-1) \\\\  & =0+(3k-3)+(-k-1) \\\\  & =2k-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle p(t), q(t) \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2k-4 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =4 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133606 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "b2d31046-ad1b-5de2-8d2b-19c2ce8b2a0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8580" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven that $x(t) = kt^2+3t$ and $y(t) = t$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(k{t}^{2}+3t)(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(k{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{k{t}^{4}}{4}+{t}^{3}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{4}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle x(t), y(t) \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{4}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{4} & =-1 \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133607 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "a9ae1458-cfaf-5a3d-a1c8-d7d539480fd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8581" :question/problem "Let $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3 x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2 x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nGiven that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/assets/b8ece1b03ba237390f26e8d47e40013abc221edcae144ca3a9dc233438d486a3.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/assets/febc66857330a11bcd7bb93952cf5b86373974f5bf87690fdcf526d4939f19ec.png) are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf x$ and $\\mathbf y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (2\\cdot k)+1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (2\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =6k-2-4 \\\\  & =6k-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 6k-6 & =0 \\\\ 6k & =6 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133608 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "f084687c-bbe3-5506-b803-ae2c7e450a8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8582" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,2\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,2\\pi\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle f(t),g(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(t) g(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t) = \\cos t$ and $g(t)=\\sin 2t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = 0$\n\n- $\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $f(t) \\perp g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity* $\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\beta=\\dfrac12(\\sin(\\alpha-\\beta)+\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle  =  \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\cos{t} \\sin2t \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{3}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )}{3}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-1-\\frac{1}{3}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-1-\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3})-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors are orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8232307-82a4-53de-bad1-9c5b618328ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308865} {:answer/id #uuid "3aedfa59-331f-5b35-b154-846f12ea70d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308866} {:answer/id #uuid "2e02c947-d75f-5244-ab5f-91bb20564642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308867} {:answer/id #uuid "a46a93a5-b2c7-5738-ab86-46da85f50f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308868} {:answer/id #uuid "d330537c-aaaf-59b8-8c4a-6fb16adf3eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a46a93a5-b2c7-5738-ab86-46da85f50f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308868} :answer-field/id #uuid "78235b23-fddb-536a-83c9-bb59105b98b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ccbba1a-31b9-5014-bee2-85dad03abf72" :question/math-academy-id "q-140263" :question/problem "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+4{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+4{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (-1\\cdot (-2))+4\\cdot (1\\cdot k) \\\\  & =-4+8+4k \\\\  & =4+4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4+4k & =0 \\\\ 4k & =-4 \\\\ k & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c75e8ba2-3603-5ca2-aab9-2a87a9acfec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132591} {:answer/id #uuid "d0080e9c-95d4-5ed3-af87-271cc9f3c19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132592} {:answer/id #uuid "09b8a0a9-e693-5862-90fd-17e1cab9583d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132593} {:answer/id #uuid "2db798ed-de8d-5f4e-8199-0378fc05ffd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132594} {:answer/id #uuid "53611b71-8da8-53bd-9ff8-777848081934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c75e8ba2-3603-5ca2-aab9-2a87a9acfec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132591} :answer-field/id #uuid "0843c47f-c932-5f31-8986-c111c47d29f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d41fa515-e181-5501-be57-d1516f0b8643" :question/math-academy-id "q-43979" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=t$ and $y(t)={t}^{2}+k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t({t}^{2}+k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{3}+kt)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{k{t}^{2}}{2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{k}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{k}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{2} & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c2c2cc5-066e-586b-913e-9616e3b08805" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308900} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4e3b27-cb43-5239-9c1d-de600b14c135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308901} {:answer/id #uuid "13f15a45-f942-5440-8896-310216bebc48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308902} {:answer/id #uuid "5b76f3cd-6843-5530-aceb-701171cf9ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308903} {:answer/id #uuid "d4566944-231f-5940-a100-61d5f7d0fd49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c4e3b27-cb43-5239-9c1d-de600b14c135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308901} :answer-field/id #uuid "9277c21b-3673-537b-9988-c4808290f327" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "02e28179-afce-5ef7-836d-53202828246c" :question/math-academy-id "q-44058" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\frac{1}{2}$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7d010c8-ba40-530d-9a14-695101bf10ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334923} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bd3c54-8f72-5c4a-94f8-7d99d8ae1f87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334924} {:answer/id #uuid "8c14b7e6-b5a2-5bb6-ab78-d643ef08b7c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334925} {:answer/id #uuid "3aeac1f8-35bb-577c-a673-b0d560acd939" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334926} {:answer/id #uuid "eea26417-b2c6-5e2e-8f81-64142aa6dedd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aeac1f8-35bb-577c-a673-b0d560acd939" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334926} :answer-field/id #uuid "b672a664-1b05-5cb7-b991-31721a57b463" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7dcbda1c-c3bc-50f6-80e4-f8f54f7d6ed6" :question/math-academy-id "q-44093" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\pi ]\\,,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=1$ and $y(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-kt){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\pi k-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =-\\pi k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\pi k & =0 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a4ee05b-d42a-549c-bcc8-69166fbb010d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334930} {:answer/id #uuid "4b8a5896-1fb8-5a9b-82cf-b18eb2d7d627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334931} {:answer/id #uuid "4050fd2a-ea89-5345-86e1-e09ad63be3cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334932} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbbcd5d-fb24-5e9a-b984-caeb61da957f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334933} {:answer/id #uuid "aed69dfb-76c5-5b4b-9e7b-4bf485c1a36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4050fd2a-ea89-5345-86e1-e09ad63be3cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334932} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae9317d9-9fe7-5129-bcbe-bc3249728a80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0664e5c-4800-551b-8dd7-364cc61ba6b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-97348" :question/problem "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$y=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =5\\cdot k+3\\cdot ((-3)\\cdot (-5))+5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5k+45+5 \\\\  & =5k+50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 5k+50 & =0 \\\\ 5k & =-50 \\\\ k & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6668b66-7151-5d8e-a195-7e463e06e4ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334937} {:answer/id #uuid "268f3139-9b38-5b60-8319-724805e58b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334938} {:answer/id #uuid "81117566-5788-52d6-b817-4c4627581ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334939} {:answer/id #uuid "65ee7943-850a-51ce-87bc-27bbf337996e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334940} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed2d0ae-68b1-5a03-87fb-0d109d6f0725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6668b66-7151-5d8e-a195-7e463e06e4ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334937} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f97e5af-98b0-5bb0-bccd-83aa27c7959b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4bf00d9-4640-52a6-b0d5-e68ac00da45c" :question/math-academy-id "q-97349" :question/problem "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=5{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =5{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (1\\cdot 2)+3\\cdot (4\\cdot 0)+(-5)\\cdot k \\\\  & =10+0-5k \\\\  & =10-5k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 10-5k & =0 \\\\ 5k & =10 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd8dc75e-a928-54dc-aff0-fda23ba3579d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334944} {:answer/id #uuid "137e6025-b0ec-5bf4-af31-49f25f365b25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334945} {:answer/id #uuid "2bcbddcc-bffe-568d-9dc0-4426060e6fb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334946} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2d1d52-0658-5175-b668-ddecf9f891b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334947} {:answer/id #uuid "ab2fa789-f446-5e31-ba9f-ed5de92c3c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb2d1d52-0658-5175-b668-ddecf9f891b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334947} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1ee84d8-54bf-5961-b98d-13904f5e0a81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62230b14-bfca-57a1-a47e-89266f3f9be7" :question/math-academy-id "q-97351" :question/problem "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ k \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 3+3\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot k)+3\\cdot (2\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =0-6k+30 \\\\  & =-6k+30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ -6k+30 & =0 \\\\ 6k & =30 \\\\ k & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cc931a4-5e07-560a-aad0-fbfc9adcee19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133281} {:answer/id #uuid "b3e78270-342c-591f-8aa5-a003848a1b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133282} {:answer/id #uuid "b4b1c128-eb64-54ef-9817-0e5ae8092cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133283} {:answer/id #uuid "1e374c1e-9a40-518f-b98e-b1f50ebd1846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133284} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7591bb-6b7b-54ff-99f3-e94a88a029e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f7591bb-6b7b-54ff-99f3-e94a88a029e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133285} :answer-field/id #uuid "242691fe-5a34-534c-bd10-15887c4f3d02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09585884-a23f-5d81-a9d8-5d380fd1cbe2" :question/math-academy-id "q-97353" :question/problem "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 5+5\\cdot ((-5)\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot (k\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =-15-75+10k \\\\  & =-90+10k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ -90+10k & =0 \\\\ 10k & =90 \\\\ k & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0a03d53-6e3c-5d7e-b39b-ef4ddd32654f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308935} {:answer/id #uuid "6aceb5ac-2608-5a7f-b4ec-4a352dfcb762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308936} {:answer/id #uuid "01084dae-567d-5a25-ab59-50bf4cc3bca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308937} {:answer/id #uuid "07b25c68-780b-5e2d-adac-9ffe36983d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308938} {:answer/id #uuid "90426f37-ed93-5189-87cc-830edda9ee48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01084dae-567d-5a25-ab59-50bf4cc3bca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308937} :answer-field/id #uuid "82b6a2b1-f5ee-532b-aadb-ffde37c868e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "131bc7fb-6fc9-5e64-83f4-c7089c6d2d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-97355" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(-4)y(-4)+x(2)y(2)+x(4)y(4).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=k-2t$ and $q(t)=5-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-4)q(-4)+p(2)q(2)+p(4)q(4) \\\\  & =(k+8)(5-8)+(k-4)(5-2)+(k-8)(5-8) \\\\  & =(-3k-24)+(3k-12)+(-3k+24) \\\\  & =-3k-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ -3k-12 & =0 \\\\ 3k & =-12 \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99acf4c2-dcec-58be-a50d-fe5d942c6be4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334951} {:answer/id #uuid "574754ee-97d6-5c7a-ad40-4bf80e4ab755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334952} {:answer/id #uuid "7b25de61-9bba-5480-9757-dc7617d6bb43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334953} {:answer/id #uuid "1974b300-3fec-5370-8fde-23f0dddb3050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334954} {:answer/id #uuid "5b3c06c1-62bd-599a-9a80-25ab1a5b6d81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1974b300-3fec-5370-8fde-23f0dddb3050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334954} :answer-field/id #uuid "df088baa-5e66-5f4b-b3d9-f10a0687ee0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a46a3b0-1c5c-5451-9298-58ec3033e122" :question/math-academy-id "q-97356" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{1}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $1,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(3)y(3).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=t-1$ and $q(t)=kt+3$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(1)q(1)+p(3)q(3) \\\\  & =(1-1)(k+3)+(3-1)(3k+3) \\\\  & =0+(6k+6) \\\\  & =6k+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 6k+6 & =0 \\\\ 6k & =-6 \\\\ k & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afb1e62a-45b7-5458-8037-cb6b0c204663" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334958} {:answer/id #uuid "d5215913-fb90-5c30-a51f-37793720faf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334959} {:answer/id #uuid "5210ba2b-28f1-51f1-8f6a-3ddfb59f0dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334960} {:answer/id #uuid "6108deba-1b88-57d9-b5a6-fc1a207fd4df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334961} {:answer/id #uuid "7114cdcc-cefb-55e2-9524-c40317aa5aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afb1e62a-45b7-5458-8037-cb6b0c204663" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334958} :answer-field/id #uuid "78eb4ef2-c0a3-503a-9a97-e5a66430627e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91fe5110-9319-5950-87cb-9762cb842dbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-97360" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1)+\\frac{1}{10}x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=3+t$ and $q(t)=3t-k{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(0)q(0)+2p(1)q(1)+\\frac{1}{10}p(2)q(2) \\\\  & =(3+0)(0)+(3+1)(3-k)+\\frac{1}{10}(3+2)(6-4k) \\\\  & =0+(12-4k)+(3-2k) \\\\  & =15-6k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 15-6k & =0 \\\\ 6k & =15 \\\\ k & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a0133d0-bf48-536a-9bbe-5a5860d7feb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334965} {:answer/id #uuid "440c51ee-f784-5b5f-8dd0-ad342804500c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334966} {:answer/id #uuid "5f5a7cca-20a7-501d-a294-132572ebdbc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334967} {:answer/id #uuid "6eeba351-4893-54ce-ab94-b35ddf37a23f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334968} {:answer/id #uuid "da4526fb-502d-5b61-a415-28d4f2ccdc89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "440c51ee-f784-5b5f-8dd0-ad342804500c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334966} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ed7e183-273b-519c-9124-c72b3330f0c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0d0f4b6-1b5a-56cd-b652-9f6d3643da68" :question/math-academy-id "q-97362" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(-1)y(-1)+x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=2+kt$ and $q(t)=t-{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1)+p(2)q(2) \\\\  & =(2-k)(-1-1)+(2+k)(1-1)+(2+2k)(2-4) \\\\  & =(-4+2k)+0+(-4-4k) \\\\  & =-8-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ -8-2k & =0 \\\\ 2k & =-8 \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39d45e59-978c-5c4a-b8ee-fb0fcbc4c57b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334972} {:answer/id #uuid "73e34b29-539c-5b1d-a149-4e4456b5d111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334973} {:answer/id #uuid "dfae4369-ca37-5c71-a0f0-1818e499717c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334974} {:answer/id #uuid "20597dcc-1d07-5d99-9ff6-962297c29789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334975} {:answer/id #uuid "1d031099-a8bc-573b-b3ec-9df97a58020f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39d45e59-978c-5c4a-b8ee-fb0fcbc4c57b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334972} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6459167-188c-5d54-8452-63d60a726e31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c1af6c8-6c3f-529c-8022-77e19d1d8408" :question/math-academy-id "q-97363" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(4)y(4)+x(-4)y(-4)+x(-5)y(-5).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=k+t$ and $q(t)=1+t-{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(4)q(4)+p(-4)q(-4)+p(-5)q(-5) \\\\  & =(k+4)(1+4-16)+(k-4)(1-4-16)+(k-5)(1-5-25) \\\\  & =(-11k-44)+(-19k+76)+(-29k+145) \\\\  & =-59k+177\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ -59k+177 & =0 \\\\ 59k & =177 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33d28332-6d03-58b4-a539-b134af21347c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334979} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0c843d-90d5-56fc-9315-e9d561e95468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334980} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6c9cdd-aea7-5136-98dd-821a993dde5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334981} {:answer/id #uuid "3d5035b8-75a6-5f6e-a545-2e8030a10491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334982} {:answer/id #uuid "12010cba-f248-5d6a-8810-51fc472e7ad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d5035b8-75a6-5f6e-a545-2e8030a10491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334982} :answer-field/id #uuid "439fbe01-aa07-580c-b35c-46beccac224d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f352e2be-b676-5530-8fa9-0bd1cab2042e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97436" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=t-2{t}^{2}$ and $y(t)=t+k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2{t}^{2})(t+k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(kt+(1-2k){t}^{2}-2{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{k{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(1-2k){t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k-1}{2}+\\frac{1-2k}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k-1}{2}+\\frac{1-2k}{3} & =0 \\\\ 3(k-1)+2(1-2k) & =0 \\\\ 3k-3+2-4k & =0 \\\\ -1-k & =0 \\\\ k & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b44b2bfe-94a6-5dce-af82-f2c4d2782d59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334986} {:answer/id #uuid "8a02e2cc-c429-5bc2-ac92-43ac33cd11d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334987} {:answer/id #uuid "433b1c9b-5e99-5fa0-9151-7402fda71c6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334988} {:answer/id #uuid "e686df33-435a-5683-9189-72d7d4adee76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334989} {:answer/id #uuid "9d4035ce-7e08-54c4-ab71-ef5115a511fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a02e2cc-c429-5bc2-ac92-43ac33cd11d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334987} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5682680-cce4-5e46-a5ae-a70fda8a4d19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ef93a42-95d6-5c7b-a187-5da2bd8f1e80" :question/math-academy-id "q-97439" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)={e}^{{t}^{2}}+k$ and $y(t)=t$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{{t}^{2}}+k)t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t{e}^{{t}^{2}}+kt)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{{t}^{2}}}{2}+\\frac{k{t}^{2}}{2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{e+k-1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{e+k-1}{2} & =0 \\\\ e+k-1 & =0 \\\\ k & =1-e\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf6fafbc-3524-5f64-b459-3698df8b35ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308956} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4d7335-3ff1-55ee-988f-ecf05889acab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308957} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bb1ca9-dd2a-5e8b-bbb0-473210b7a8d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308958} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7789c0-1483-5818-b458-87586c50ba3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308959} {:answer/id #uuid "77723d8b-97e0-5ecf-a784-4013011ecd3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf6fafbc-3524-5f64-b459-3698df8b35ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308956} :answer-field/id #uuid "23b75cfa-37eb-56b3-9b86-260ea837f3d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "579248ee-641f-5ef7-a9f1-c830a24589fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-97440" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=5{t}^{2}-2$ and $y(t)=k+3{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(5{t}^{2}-2)(k+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{t}^{4}+(5k-6){t}^{2}-2k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(3{t}^{5}+\\frac{(5k-6){t}^{3}}{3}-2kt){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{(5k-6)}{3}-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3+\\frac{(5k-6)}{3}-2k & =0 \\\\ 9+(5k-6)-6k & =0 \\\\ 3-k & =0 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67cd2901-db7d-5a1f-ae27-2ba0b38c96a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308970} {:answer/id #uuid "9c34072c-5988-5515-a7ce-d328e36106f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308971} {:answer/id #uuid "e587b8c6-83f9-5d79-b369-b7791276bbf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308972} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a2d029-3159-577d-9e55-4e3451b7c4ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308973} {:answer/id #uuid "f64bcfd0-22b7-5d04-b678-faecdf9a7255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e587b8c6-83f9-5d79-b369-b7791276bbf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308972} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6030c2d-9773-598e-b8be-79fa3d4c9985" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6302273c-fd2b-591e-9cb8-0335b24472a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-97445" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,3]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,3],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)={e}^{t},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2{e}^{3}+1$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=1$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{3}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{3}-{\\int }_{0}^{3}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t{e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{3}-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(3{e}^{3}-0{e}^{0})-{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =3{e}^{3}-({e}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "722f90dc-693d-5e9b-9255-26611732b9f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334993} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb1a7da-962d-5399-b600-a33603a0219e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334994} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0206e4-cb92-5d1b-b5f4-dd7c4fd6df0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334995} {:answer/id #uuid "c838fff2-b351-501c-ab8c-8e747a5ab545" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334996} {:answer/id #uuid "6203d3e4-bcc8-5cab-a551-d201f4f09991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeb1a7da-962d-5399-b600-a33603a0219e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334994} :answer-field/id #uuid "18b12b2c-0967-524d-a541-72ba430022aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dec8198d-dc17-54f1-8d34-81db052f9f8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97448" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,0]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,0],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)=\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=-\\frac{1}{3}$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}t\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=1-{t}^{2},$ $\\text{d}u=-2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}t\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3/2}]-[-\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aab0db5a-5463-5c63-9f36-b1f43b5a48c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133305} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa7b3bb-9bd5-5178-b7e8-a5f43f2879d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133306} {:answer/id #uuid "0938b586-0bc2-5b6d-89fb-5600409406bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133307} {:answer/id #uuid "17fe6c4c-efd0-515f-8425-1da3e6fa4edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133308} {:answer/id #uuid "72eca67c-b54b-5a37-9cfc-f3bde7962300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aab0db5a-5463-5c63-9f36-b1f43b5a48c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133305} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d07abe0-c51c-58fb-bc68-8fc7c6635e54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c169d7e-31f7-5721-948b-24c63fd291d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-97451" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}{4}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\pi )}{4}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d23d3cf-f562-55b6-a1a1-1b4a4146c492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335000} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd49d25-d5cb-5d5d-80a1-41b98bc33285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335001} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2a84b8-6764-5574-beb4-96645fee1169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335002} {:answer/id #uuid "0d0841b6-2d1d-5dab-a19e-108eb773ab25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335003} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f0080d-47c8-51b3-bd21-ac49faf4fffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7f0080d-47c8-51b3-bd21-ac49faf4fffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335004} :answer-field/id #uuid "85cda8ba-6b7d-5413-8098-1e67d942ffc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b51342e3-c370-5f22-a8a0-d957bb6d3391" :question/math-academy-id "q-97452" :question/problem "Let $V=C[1,{e}^{2}]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[1,{e}^{2}],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\frac{1}{t},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
